{"y":2004,"label":"2003–04","coach":"Jim Calhoun","conf":"Big East","confShort":"Big East","w":33,"l":6,"cw":12,"cl":4,"confFinish":"2nd in Big East","finish":"champ","seed":2,"region":"Phoenix","apPre":1,"apHigh":1,"apFinal":7,"srs":22.64,"sos":7.77,"ortg":112.1,"drtg":90.9,"ppg":78.8,"oppg":63.9,"story":{"headline":"Calhoun's Best Team Wins It All","text":"Preseason No. 1 with its whole starting lineup back, UConn stumbled against Georgia Tech in the Preseason NIT, then won 11 straight, including an 86–59 rout of No. 6 Oklahoma at Gampel. The Huskies finished second in the Big East, then won the tournament, beating top-seeded Pittsburgh 61–58 in the final as Ben Gordon set a scoring record and took MVP honors. As a No. 2 seed they beat Vermont, DePaul, Vanderbilt and Alabama — Rashad Anderson hit six threes and scored 28 in the regional final — then rallied from eight down with three minutes left to stun Duke 79–78 in the Final Four. On April 5 in San Antonio, UConn beat Georgia Tech 82–73 for its second national title. Emeka Okafor, battling back problems all season, was Most Outstanding Player, Big East Player of the Year and national defensive player of the year. A night later the UConn women also won, making UConn the first school to sweep both titles. Okafor and Gordon went No. 2 and No. 3 in the draft; Calhoun called it the best team he ever coached.","moments":[{"date":"2004-03-13","title":"Big East tournament Championship game: W vs Pittsburgh","text":"UConn beat Pittsburgh 61–58 (Championship game) at Madison Square Garden, New York."},{"date":"2004-03-18","title":"NCAA First Round: W vs Vermont","text":"UConn beat Vermont 70–53 in the NCAA tournament first round at HSBC Arena, Buffalo, New York."},{"date":"2004-03-20","title":"NCAA Second Round: W vs DePaul","text":"UConn beat DePaul 72–55 in the NCAA tournament second round at HSBC Arena, Buffalo, New York."},{"date":"2004-03-25","title":"NCAA Sweet Sixteen: W vs Vanderbilt","text":"UConn beat Vanderbilt 73–53 in the NCAA tournament sweet sixteen at America West Arena, Phoenix, Arizona."},{"date":"2004-03-27","title":"NCAA Elite Eight: W vs Alabama","text":"UConn beat Alabama 87–71 in the NCAA tournament elite eight at America West Arena, Phoenix, Arizona."},{"date":"2004-04-03","title":"Comeback vs Duke","text":"Down eight with three minutes left, UConn rallies to beat Duke 79–78 in the Final Four."},{"date":"2004-04-05","title":"Second national title","text":"UConn beats Georgia Tech 82–73 in San Antonio; Emeka Okafor is Most Outstanding Player."}],"honors":["NCAA national champions","Big East tournament champions","Emeka Okafor – NCAA Final Four Most Outstanding Player","Emeka Okafor – Consensus first-team All-American","Emeka Okafor – NABC National Co-Player of the Year","Emeka Okafor – Pete Newell Big Man Award","Emeka Okafor – NABC Defensive Player of the Year","Emeka Okafor – Academic All-American of the Year","Emeka Okafor – Big East Player of the Year","Emeka Okafor – Big East Defensive Player of the Year","Ben Gordon – Big East Tournament MVP","Ben Gordon – NCAA Regional Most Outstanding Player","Emeka Okafor – First-team All-Big East","Ben Gordon – First-team All-Big East"],"sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2003%E2%80%9304_Connecticut_Huskies_men's_basketball_team","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/UConn_Huskies_men%27s_basketball","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jim_Calhoun","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emeka_Okafor","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ben_Gordon","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rashad_Anderson"]},"roster":[{"pid":"ben-gordon-1","name":"Ben Gordon","num":"4","cls":"JR","pos":"G","ht":"6-2","wt":195,"home":"Mount Vernon, NY","rsci":"41 ( 2001 )","photo":"assets/players/ben-gordon-1.jpg?v=0bc44b6d","photoWide":true,"pg":{"g":39,"gs":39,"mp":34.5,"pts":18.5,"trb":4.7,"ast":4.5,"stl":1.4,"blk":0.2,"tov":2.8,"fg_pct":0.434,"fg3_pct":0.433,"ft_pct":0.829,"orb":0.9,"pf":2.3},"tot":{"mp":1346,"fg":239,"fga":551,"fg3":104,"fg3a":240,"ft":141,"f